/*
 * Linker version script for the statically linked build (see config.m4).
 *
 * A static link pulls liblbug's object files into our .so, and the linker re-exports every
 * global symbol it takes — 2712 of them, including 130 STB_GNU_UNIQUE locale facet ids from
 * libstdc++'s template instantiations. glibc binds unique symbols process-wide and ignores
 * RTLD_DEEPBIND, so re-exporting those would make this extension the first half of exactly
 * the crash it was built to avoid: the next library to load would bind its locale registry
 * to our copy instead of the system libstdc++'s. tools/repro-install-crash-dlopen.c has the
 * mechanism in full.
 *
 * Hiding everything is not an option either. LadybugDB's own extensions — json, fts, vector —
 * are shared objects downloaded by `INSTALL` and dlopened by liblbug, and they resolve 91
 * `lbug::` C++ symbols from whoever loaded liblbug. With no exports, `LOAD json` fails with
 * "undefined symbol: _ZTIN4lbug8function17TableFuncBindDataE".
 *
 * So the split is by owner rather than by language: everything belonging to liblbug stays
 * visible, everything belonging to the C++ standard library does not. `*4lbug*` is a glob over
 * mangled names and catches the C++ API, its typeinfo, vtables and thunks, plus the std::
 * containers instantiated over liblbug's own types (std::_Hashtable<lbug::common::int128_t…>)
 * — those describe liblbug state and its extensions have to share them. What it excludes is
 * the part with no liblbug type in it, which is where the locale facets live.
 *
 * Measured on Debian arm64, liblbug 0.19.1: 281 -> 35 std:: exports, 23 -> 6 unique, and the
 * six that remain are liblbug's own statics (StringUtils::asciiToLowerCaseMap and friends).
 *
 * Linux only. macOS uses a two-level namespace, where a library's symbols are not offered to
 * the rest of the process at all, so the hazard does not exist and no filtering is needed.
 */
{
  global:
    get_module;
    lbug_*;
    *4lbug*;
  local:
    *;
};
